nodecode-osv is a rewrite of EloquentBrush0x's EloquentBrush0x/hermes-context-refs:
https://github.com/EloquentBrush0x/hermes-context-refs
commit 43ab2b75e660db5edcfe2f215776294c45408b40
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 EloquentBrush0x (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's one subject -- a vulnerability record from
https://api.osv.dev/v1/vulns/<id>, with CVE, GHSA and other OSV ids
normalized the way it normalized them, up to four alias records read for the
package data a CVE record lacks, and the record rendered as the same block of
text (summary, aliases, dates, severity vectors with their source records,
weaknesses, affected packages with version ranges and fixed versions,
upstream git ranges when no record has package data, details, ranked
references, and the closing note that advisory text is quoted material). The
id checks, the character cap on the details, the 20 affected entries, the 12
references and the refusal wording follow the original. The original's
settings, max_chars and timeout_seconds, are kept, and alias_lookups replaces
its fixed limit of four.

Left out: the "@cve:", "@ghsa:" and "@osv:" prefixes themselves. Those are
Hermes Agent's context-reference extension point, which Nodecode has no
equivalent of; here the same lookup is the osv_lookup tool, the /osv command
and the (osv:lookup ...) verb. Also left out: the parallel alias reads (they
run one at a time under the same deadline, so a slow OSV shortens the alias
list instead of holding the turn), the same-host-only redirect rule (the
image's HTTP client follows at most five redirects), and autocomplete, which
in the original answered nothing anyway.
